**Proof technique:** Construct $Q_w,R_w$ on words by moving $E_i$ to the right, prove the defining identity on words by induction, and obtain existence and uniqueness for all elements from [F1] and the linear independence of the toral coefficients.

1.1 For each word $w=F_{j_1}\cdots F_{j_m}$ define $Q_w,R_w\in U_q^-$ by recursion on $m$: for the empty word $Q_1:=R_1:=0$, and for $m\ge1$, with $w'=F_{j_2}\cdots F_{j_m}$, set $Q_w:=q_i^{a_{ij_1}}F_{j_1}Q_{w'}+\delta_{ij_1}w'$ and $R_w:=q_i^{-a_{ij_1}}F_{j_1}R_{w'}+\delta_{ij_1}w'$. Each is a finite $\mathbb Q(q)$-linear combination of words. If $w$ has degree $-
u$, every nonzero term of $Q_w,R_w$ has degree $-
u+\alpha_i$: inductively a nonzero $Q_{w'},R_{w'}$ has degree $\deg w'+\alpha_i$, multiplication by $F_{j_1}$ gives $\deg w+\alpha_i$, and the term $\delta_{ij_1}w'$ has that degree when $j_1=i$. [given, F1, F2, construct]

2.1 For every word $w$ one has $E_iw-wE_i=(K_iQ_w-K_i^{-1}R_w)/(q_i-q_i^{-1})$. For $m=0$ both sides vanish. For $m\ge1$, write $w=F_{j_1}w'$. Multiplying the inductive identity for $w'$ by $F_{j_1}$ on the left gives $F_{j_1}E_iw'=F_{j_1}w'E_i+F_{j_1}K_iQ_{w'}/(q_i-q_i^{-1})-F_{j_1}K_i^{-1}R_{w'}/(q_i-q_i^{-1})$, and [F3] gives $E_iF_{j_1}-F_{j_1}E_i=\delta_{ij_1}(K_i-K_i^{-1})/(q_i-q_i^{-1})$. Adding, and using [F2] in the form $F_{j_1}K_i=q_i^{a_{ij_1}}K_iF_{j_1}$, $F_{j_1}K_i^{-1}=q_i^{-a_{ij_1}}K_i^{-1}F_{j_1}$, yields $E_iw-wE_i= igl(K_i(q_i^{a_{ij_1}}F_{j_1}Q_{w'}+\delta_{ij_1}w')-K_i^{-1}(q_i^{-a_{ij_1}}F_{j_1}R_{w'}+\delta_{ij_1}w') igr)/(q_i-q_i^{-1})=(K_iQ_w-K_i^{-1}R_w)/(q_i-q_i^{-1})$, which is the claim. [step 1.1, F2, F3, algebra]

